1
00:00:00,760 --> 00:00:03,020
Your computer can't
actually do anything.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:04,740
Not the way you think.

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:09,200
Every photo, song and game
on it comes down to one

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:12,320
tiny trick, repeated
over and over.

5
00:00:13,020 --> 00:00:16,000
Inside the chip are billions
of tiny parts called

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:16,800
transistors.

7
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:21,580
Each one holds a single
answer, like a yes or

8
00:00:21,580 --> 00:00:22,380
a no.

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,360
Eight of them in a row
can stand for any one

10
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:28,320
of two hundred and fifty-six
things, like a letter,

11
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:30,220
a colour, or a number.

12
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:33,860
Line up enough of them, and
you can describe a song,

13
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:35,320
or a face.

14
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:38,680
Change them fast enough,
and the picture moves.

15
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:40,440
So what's the trick?

16
00:00:41,220 --> 00:00:42,860
Each transistor is a switch.

17
00:00:43,780 --> 00:00:46,200
And all your computer can
do is tell whether a switch

18
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:47,660
is on, or off.

19
00:00:49,206 --> 00:00:51,406
Two thousand three
hundred switches.

20
00:00:52,366 --> 00:00:54,546
That's all the first
computer chip you could buy

21
00:00:54,546 --> 00:00:55,346
had.

22
00:00:55,686 --> 00:00:58,686
The chips we use now have
far more, and the real

23
00:00:58,686 --> 00:01:00,286
number is hard to even picture.

24
00:01:01,086 --> 00:01:04,906
That first chip was
Intel's 4004, from 1971.

25
00:01:05,946 --> 00:01:09,046
Each of its switches, called
a transistor, is simply

26
00:01:09,046 --> 00:01:10,026
on or off.

27
00:01:10,786 --> 00:01:13,946
Say you tried to count them,
one every second, without

28
00:01:13,946 --> 00:01:14,746
ever sleeping.

29
00:01:15,466 --> 00:01:17,526
Two thousand three hundred
would take you under

30
00:01:17,526 --> 00:01:18,326
an hour.

31
00:01:18,806 --> 00:01:21,146
A billion would take
you over thirty years.

32
00:01:21,986 --> 00:01:24,366
Even an everyday laptop
chip has billions.

33
00:01:25,346 --> 00:01:28,886
And the biggest yet,
Apple's M2 Ultra from 2023,

34
00:01:29,446 --> 00:01:32,486
has a hundred and thirty-four
billion, about fifty-eight

35
00:01:32,486 --> 00:01:34,026
million times the first.

36
00:01:34,906 --> 00:01:37,546
Counting those would take
you more than four thousand

37
00:01:37,546 --> 00:01:38,346
years.

38
00:01:39,539 --> 00:01:43,219
Grace Hopper carried a
nanosecond in her pocket.

39
00:01:43,919 --> 00:01:46,339
And it shows something
strange about the computer

40
00:01:46,339 --> 00:01:47,719
you're using right now.

41
00:01:48,239 --> 00:01:51,799
Hopper was a computer pioneer
and a US Navy admiral.

42
00:01:52,599 --> 00:01:55,859
At her talks, she handed
out pieces of wire about

43
00:01:55,859 --> 00:01:57,299
thirty centimetres long.

44
00:01:58,039 --> 00:02:02,199
That, she said, is a
nanosecond, a billionth

45
00:02:02,199 --> 00:02:05,219
of a second: the farthest
light can travel in

46
00:02:05,219 --> 00:02:06,019
that time.

47
00:02:06,639 --> 00:02:10,139
A microsecond was a coil
three hundred metres long.

48
00:02:10,959 --> 00:02:13,439
She joked that programmers
should hang one around

49
00:02:13,439 --> 00:02:15,779
their neck, to see
what they were wasting.

50
00:02:16,619 --> 00:02:20,379
Now, a processor running
at three gigahertz takes

51
00:02:20,379 --> 00:02:23,159
one step every third
of a nanosecond.

52
00:02:23,959 --> 00:02:25,659
So cut her wire into three.

53
00:02:26,459 --> 00:02:29,099
In one tick of your
computer's clock, light,

54
00:02:29,099 --> 00:02:32,959
the fastest thing there
is, only gets about ten

55
00:02:32,959 --> 00:02:33,759
centimetres.

56
00:02:34,439 --> 00:02:36,359
Roughly the width of your hand.

57
00:02:38,353 --> 00:02:41,153
Your processor spends a lot
of its time doing nothing

58
00:02:41,153 --> 00:02:41,953
at all.

59
00:02:42,313 --> 00:02:44,433
It isn't broken,
and it isn't lazy.

60
00:02:45,133 --> 00:02:47,773
And engineers have some
clever tricks to stop it.

61
00:02:48,353 --> 00:02:50,913
A processor can finish
a step in a fraction of

62
00:02:50,913 --> 00:02:51,733
a nanosecond.

63
00:02:52,453 --> 00:02:54,753
That's so fast that the
rest of the computer often

64
00:02:54,753 --> 00:02:55,613
can't keep up.

65
00:02:56,413 --> 00:02:59,713
The information it needs
is usually stored somewhere

66
00:02:59,713 --> 00:03:03,173
else, in memory, and
fetching it takes time, even

67
00:03:03,173 --> 00:03:04,293
at the speed of electricity.

68
00:03:05,293 --> 00:03:06,093
So it waits.

69
00:03:06,813 --> 00:03:07,733
Here are the tricks.

70
00:03:08,573 --> 00:03:10,993
Small stores of memory
right next to the processor,

71
00:03:11,273 --> 00:03:14,113
called caches, keep what
it's likely to need close

72
00:03:14,113 --> 00:03:14,913
by.

73
00:03:15,293 --> 00:03:18,313
And it guesses what it
will need next, and starts

74
00:03:18,313 --> 00:03:19,113
early.

75
00:03:19,613 --> 00:03:22,553
A lot of what makes a chip
fast is simply waiting

76
00:03:22,553 --> 00:03:23,353
less.

77
00:03:35,989 --> 00:03:37,649
Why does your
computer need a fan?

78
00:03:38,489 --> 00:03:40,629
The heat comes from
somewhere you can't see.

79
00:03:41,309 --> 00:03:44,609
Inside the chip are billions
of tiny switches, called

80
00:03:44,609 --> 00:03:45,489
transistors.

81
00:03:46,389 --> 00:03:49,189
They flip on and off,
up to billions of times

82
00:03:49,189 --> 00:03:51,829
a second, whenever your
computer does anything at

83
00:03:51,829 --> 00:03:52,629
all.

84
00:03:52,809 --> 00:03:55,849
Each flip takes a tiny
bit of electricity.

85
00:03:56,809 --> 00:03:58,189
On its own, that's nothing.

86
00:03:58,909 --> 00:04:01,869
But billions of switches,
billions of times a second,

87
00:04:02,289 --> 00:04:02,729
add up.

88
00:04:02,729 --> 00:04:05,749
And that electricity doesn't
get stored away anywhere.

89
00:04:06,589 --> 00:04:08,289
Almost all of it
ends up as heat.

90
00:04:09,029 --> 00:04:11,289
So the fan, the metal
fins and the little pipes

91
00:04:11,289 --> 00:04:13,629
are there to carry it
away, before the chip has

92
00:04:13,629 --> 00:04:14,749
to slow itself down.

93
00:04:15,529 --> 00:04:18,289
When your fan spins up, that's
the sound of arithmetic.

94
00:04:20,501 --> 00:04:22,821
Your computer has never
seen a single photo.

95
00:04:22,821 --> 00:04:26,621
Not even the ones it
shows you every day.

96
00:04:27,301 --> 00:04:30,741
All it does is shuffle
patterns of on and off, very,

97
00:04:30,981 --> 00:04:31,781
very fast.

98
00:04:32,461 --> 00:04:34,921
When you open a photo,
the chip isn't looking at

99
00:04:34,921 --> 00:04:35,721
a face.

100
00:04:36,201 --> 00:04:39,081
It's moving numbers that
say how bright each tiny

101
00:04:39,081 --> 00:04:40,941
dot on your screen should be.

102
00:04:41,661 --> 00:04:44,241
When you play a song,
it's sending numbers to

103
00:04:44,241 --> 00:04:45,041
a speaker.

104
00:04:45,521 --> 00:04:48,861
The switches don't know
what a face is, or a song,

105
00:04:49,421 --> 00:04:50,221
or a joke.

106
00:04:50,721 --> 00:04:51,521
They just flip.

107
00:04:52,241 --> 00:04:53,581
So where does the
meaning come from?

108
00:04:54,361 --> 00:04:56,681
It only happens when
the pattern reaches you.

109
00:04:57,401 --> 00:04:59,721
All the meaning is on
your side of the screen.
